        ":question/id": "c05827a9-fdcf-58d8-bfea-b9a09196b4ea",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-48081",
        ":question/problem": "Select the function that is continuous at $x=2.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The correct answer is shown below.  Indeed, the graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=2$ and it would be impossible to draw them in a neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking the pencil off of the paper."
      },
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186233061,
        ":question/difficulty": {
          ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
        },
        ":question/id": "62ec00bb-a7dc-5ae8-ae64-a716f57687a2",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-102106",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/d9/d9cf9a5c61cbf1796cf6682b129b4c05171c364c7b34e7cce176ba27e9c9cb24.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-1$\nII. - $x=2$\nIII. - $x=4$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=2$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=4.$ Since there is an asymptote at $x=4,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"I and II only\".\n\n![](images/95/95967eb82512aae261936a6c09e2f474c72c9a130d3ecb527f62408822d88c96.png)"
      },
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186234403,
        ":question/difficulty": {
          ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/moderate"
        },
        ":question/id": "b9857052-b60b-5b90-a22b-cd84e32dabbe",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-78133",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/43/43544a1ba403055094cae54fed9772df353d3697f50888728ebee61e77b729da.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\nI. - $x=-7$\nII. - $x=-4$\nIII. - $x=-1$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-7.$ Because there is a jump at $x=-7,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-7$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-4.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=-4$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a break at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is: \"II only\"."
      },
