        ":question/id": "6b614a15-6e39-5d82-8606-db6fffa56cff",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-48082",
        ":question/problem": "Select the function that is continuous at $x=0.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The correct answer is shown below.  Indeed, despite the fact that the function has a discontinuity at $x=2$, the graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](images/90/90c2fbe99d0b202e09244084d2501608018e030e6660a225f708cbc6b462dccd.png)\n\n   \n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=0$ and it would be impossible to draw them in a neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking the pencil off of the paper."
